> GroupPars
>     MEAN1 COV11
> par      0  1.022

>
> If you see there is change in the values of the parameter d. I have not changed the response pattern (or number of examinees) for first three items in both calibration.

>
> Now I have a little bit more questions this time. :)
>
> 1) Why there is a change in the values of the parameter?

The model is different. IRT is a multivariate technique, so when you add items in you change the definition of the θ parameters (generally measuring it better), as well as fit more coefficients with more degrees of freedom (here, 2^3=8 versus 2^5 = 32, total df, while fitting 4 versus 6 coefs).

>
> 2.A) Another question which I have is that suppose I already have calibrated item bank of K items. I get M (K>M) new uncalibrated items in future. Now what should be the good idea for calibrating these new items?

You could either refit everything, or treat the previous parameters as fixed/population values in the model and simply estimate the unknown coefs. Both approaches will work for estimating parameter variability by computing the standard errors.

>
> 2.B) Should we calibrate them separately? If yes what is the minimum number of items and responses required to get the robust item parameter estimates?

Unclear, it's situation dependent. Do what's best given what you know and have.

>
> 2.C) Or should we calibrate them together? ( All K+M items are going together for calibration) ( but that might change the first K  items' parameter estimates )
>
> E.g. Suppose In a year I get some new items getting added to my item bank. So should I calibrate these M items at the end of the year when I have all the items.

It's generally a good idea to update your item parameters as time goes on for consistency. I'd recommend investigating what larger testing corporations like ETS do to keep their items valid and fresh.

>
> Or I can go for the calibration as the items get added one by one (may be calibrating first  3 items, then second 3 and last 4 or first 5 and last 5) ?
>
> My aim is to fetch the reliable estimates of item parameters.
>
> 3)  Last but not the least, Suppose I keep getting the more new responses to the already calibrated items ( through CAT (Computerized Adaptive Test)). These responses will be sparse as it is a CAT. Should I keep updating the item parameters by re-calibrating the items as long as I keep getting the new responses? Is it right that more the the responses better are item parameter estimates? Or should I stop at some point? If yes, what is that stopping criteria, where one can say that my item parameter estimates are robust?

Add in more response patterns to an IRT analysis will always improve the accuracy of the parameters (regardless of whether they are sparse or not), provided the new responses are from the same population. Stopping criteria is up to you: how precise do you need your estimates to be, and does it even matter if you have a relatively strict CAT stopping criteria? Again, checking out CAT texts is a good idea, since they are slightly outside my expertise at this moment. Cheers.

Phil

